.th PXP I 4/1/77
.sh NAME
pxp \*- Pascal execution profiler
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd pxp
[
.bd \*-cdfimntuwxy0248\*_
] [
.bd \*-z
[ section ... ] ] file [ pmon.out ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
This document is a quick reference to
.it pxp
usage.  For an introduction
and more explanation see the ``PXP User Manual.''
.s3
.it Pxp
is a versatile program which can serve at least three distinct
purposes.  It can be used to produce execution profiles of
Pascal programs,
to clean up the source text of programs,
and as an aid to syntactic debugging.
.s3
.it Pxp
can produce an execution profile of a program after
a program has been compiled with the \fB\*-z\fR option
enabled to
.it "pc (I)"
and the object has been executed.
At process termination, the file \fIpmon.out\fR is created by
the Pascal runtime system,
containing information giving the number of times each statement
in the program was executed.  The command:
.s3
.dt
.bd "	pxp \*-z name.p"
.s3
then takes information present in the \fIpmon.out\fR
file and merges it with a structured listing of the program.
If the monitor data is not in the file \fIpmon.out\fR
in the current directory,
then the name of the data file must be given after the
source program file name.
.s3
More specifically
.it pxp
is in
profile mode
if any of the \fB\*-z\fR, \fB\*-m\fR, or \fB\*-c\fR options are given on the
command line.  In this case 
.it pxp
will print the header line of each procedure of function in the program
giving an execution count.  The bodies of functions and procedures are
also printed if the \fBz\fR option is on.  See also the descriptions
of the \fB\*-n\fR and \fB\*-0\fR options which affect this.  The \fB\*-t\fR
option sets profile mode but only causes the generation of a summary table.
See the ``PXP User Manual'' for examples.
.s3
To use
.it pxp
to reformat Pascal programs, one specifies 
neither a profile data file name nor the \fB\*-t\fR,
\fB\*-z\fR,
\fB\*-y\fR or
\fB\*-m\fR
flags.
This produces a structured
version of the program on the standard output in a format suitable
for resubmission to
.it pc.
.it Pxp
differs from many ``pretty printers'' in that it actually
parses the entire program, using the same parser as 
.it "pc (I)."
It then walks the generated parse tree and, as its
semantic actions, formats a program.  Thus, it can be used to see
what the true structure of the program is.
.s3
.it Pxp
can be used to syntactically debug a program.
By using the \fB\*-y\fR option, one can obtain
listings of the source lines which contain syntactic errors and
reformatted listings of only the containing \fIunits\fR,
giving the structure
which
.it pxp
could discern.
This feature of
.it pxp
is still under development.
.s3
The following flags are interpreted by
.it pxp.
For some of these flags the options can also be controlled in
comments within the program text.  See the ``User Manual,''
``PXP User Manual,'' and ``Report Appendix'' for details.
The
\fB\*-c\fR,
\fB\*-m\fR,
and \fB\*-t\fR
options
are distinct from those with the same letters in
the text of the program.
These flags as well as all non-alphabetic flags are 
have effect only on the command line.
.s3
.dt
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-c\fR	Extract profile data from the file 
\fIcore\fR rather than from \fIpmon.out\fR.
File names other than \fIcore\fR may be given in the same way
as names other than \fIpmon.out\fR are given.
Defaults off, a command line only flag.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-d\fR	Omit declaration parts.
Defaults off, but set on by \fB\*-z\fR, \fB\*-m\fR and \fB\*-c\fR.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-f\fR	Fully parenthesize expressions.
The default is off and gives minimum
parenthesization necessary to preserve the structure of the input
text.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-i\fR	Indent nested procedures and functions.  Default is on.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-m\fR	Turns on profiling without enabling the \fBz\fR option flag.
It is most
useful when the program already has embedded \fBz\fR option control or only the
procedure/function header lines are wanted.
Default is off, a command line only flag.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-n\fR	Suppresses even the first line of procedures and functions
when the \fBz\fR option is off. Default is off.
.br
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-t\fR	Print a table summarizing the procedures and functions and the
number of times each was called. Default is off, a command line only flag.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-u\fR	72 column option.
Default is off.
See
.it "pc (I)."
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-w\fR	Warning message option.
Default is on.
See
.it "pc (I)."
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-y\fR	List only syntactic errors and \fIunits\fR in which they occur.
Default is off, a command line only flag.  Under development.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-z\fR	Controls printing of the execution profile.
Default is off.
Giving the \fB\*-z\fR option on the command line not only puts
.it pxp
into profile mode
but it also sets the \fBz\fR option so that, effectively, all of the program
will be profiled.  If the \fB\*-z\fR option is followed by a list of section
names (procedures/functions) then instead of
enabling the profile at the beginning
of the program,
the effect of the \fB\*-z\fR option is to
enable profiling during the bodies of those
procedures or functions only.
Details and examples are given in the ``PXP User Manual.''
.i0
.s3
The following non-alphabetic options are command line only.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-\*_\fR	Underline keywords. Default is off.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-\fRd	Where \fId\fR
is one of the digits 2, 4, or 8 sets the number of spaces
of indenting per block level.  Default is 4.
.s3
.lp +6 5
\fB\*-0\fR	When profiling, suppress the bodies of functions and procedures
with 0 counts. Default is on.
.i0
.dt
.sh FILES
pmon.out	profile data
.br
core		for profile data when \fB\*-c\fR option given.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
UNIX Pascal ``User Manual,'' ``Report Appendix,'' and ``PXP User Manual,''
pc (I),
pcx (I),
pcc (I),
pxref (I)
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
.lp +6 6
\fB"core": no profile data\fR
.br
The \fB\*-m\fR option was not enabled to 
.it "pc (I)."
.s3
.lp +6 6
\fB"core": bad format\fR
.br
Most likely the \fIcore\fR is not a \fIcore\fR from 
.it "pcx (I)."
.s3
.lp +6 6
\fBcounter mismatch\fR
.br
Occurs when the number of procedures and functions or the number
of counters in the monitor data disagree with the number expected
for the program, usually because the wrong program is specified
or the program has been changed since it was compiled.
.i0
.sh AUTHOR
William Joy
.sh BUGS
Comments are not yet preserved.
.s3
